in @prism

in @metalogue

in @code

in @glass

in @nl

# @code/metalogue — the AST speaking to itself.
#
# The 34th-instance recognition (2026-06-09, Reed + Alex → Mara):
# `@code/macro` was the language-community word for what the
# substrate already names. `@metalogue` (shards/metalogue.mirror,
# B3) declared the natural-language self-conversation per Bateson
# 1972: *a conversation whose structure reflects its topic*. The
# code generation surface is the same shape lifted to the AST
# altitude — the language speaking ABOUT itself BY USING itself,
# at compile time, through its own metaprogramming surface.
#
# `@code/metalogue` is the altitude-parametric specialization of
# `@metalogue`: where `@metalogue` carries `nl` turn bodies,
# `@code/metalogue` carries `declaration` turn bodies. Both ground
# the metalogue discipline — different altitudes, same shape. The
# substrate had the word; this shard names the inheritance.
#
# The per-species realisations (`@code/rust/macro`,
# `@code/elixir/macro`, `@code/lisp/macro`, etc.) keep their
# community-vocabulary name ("macro") at the species altitude
# because that's the word language users recognize. The substrate
# gains `@code/metalogue` as the universal ground; species inherit
# via `<= @code/metalogue` per
# `[[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]]`. The realisations
# bind the universal contract to per-language AST types
# (`code/rust.ast`, `code/elixir.ast`, ...) — the typed surface is
# species-local; the contract is ground.
#
# === why metalogue, not macro ===
#
# Three readings converge on the lift:
#
# 1. **Substrate-already-had-the-word.** Per
#    `[[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]]` (the 33-instance
#    recognition track), each "new" concept turns out to be a name
#    the substrate was implicitly using. `@metalogue` was declared
#    2026-06-05 (B3, task #189) for the NL altitude. The macro
#    surface IS that same Bateson-1972 self-conversation at the
#    AST altitude. Naming it `@code/metalogue` makes the lineage
#    structural rather than coincidental.
#
# 2. **Bateson 1972 made literal at compile-time.** A metalogue's
#    structure reflects its topic. A macro's emission IS the
#    structure of its substrate input. The shim is a typed turn
#    in the conversation: the substrate utters a `declaration`;
#    the language's macro layer hears it and emits the realisation;
#    the language compiler closes the loop. Per the spec's §5 ("the
#    gap is the spec — formalized"), every `\` body is the
#    substrate's question; every realisation is the language's
#    answer; together they are the metalogue.
#
# 3. **`@metalogue` declared `transparency(turn)` as the residual
#    opacity carrier.** The macro shim's four laws (round-trip,
#    OID-functionality, type-soundness, substrate-pull preservation)
#    are obligations on the conversation's transparency — they
#    name what MUST close so the conversation doesn't silently
#    absorb the gap. Per
#    `[[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]]`, the opacity
#    surfaces here as `transparency(turn)` over
#    `metalogue_session.turns`.
#
# === inheritance shape: explicit `in @metalogue` and `in @code` ===
#
# `@code/metalogue` IS-A `@metalogue` (lifted to the AST altitude)
# AND IS-A part-of `@code` (the per-altitude grammar discipline).
# Both inheritances are declared with `in @X` per the substrate's
# canonical import-and-inherit form (cf. `shards/code.mirror`'s
# discipline of `in @prism` + `in @meta` + `in @nl` + `in @glass`).
# The path-namespace property (`shards/code/metalogue.mirror`
# declares `@code/metalogue`) makes the family relationship
# structural; the explicit `in @metalogue` makes the discipline
# inheritance structural too.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/code-metalogue-surface.md (the renamed,
# reframed spec — the contract is now declared HERE at the ground,
# not at the species). Substrate decisions:
# [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
# [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
# [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]],
# [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]].
# Related shards: metalogue.mirror (the NL-altitude sibling, B3),
#                 code.mirror (the family root),
#                 code/rust/macro.mirror (the canonical Rust
#                 realisation; declares `<= @code/metalogue`),
#                 code/mirror.mirror (the sibling render surface at
#                 T17 — `render` is one half of the codegen pipeline;
#                 `metalogue` reception is the other).

prism @code/metalogue {
  focus metalogue
  project metalogue
  split metalogue
  shift metalogue
  settle metalogue
}

# === The four substrate-declaration kinds ===
#
# Per docs/specs/code-metalogue-surface.md §4.1, every substrate
# declaration that crosses the glass wall to a language realisation
# is one of four kinds. The shim's dispatch is on this kind; the
# kind is universal substrate vocabulary, not language-specific.
#
# - type     — substrate `type T { ... }` or `type T = v1 | v2`
# - prism    — substrate `prism @X { five-op block }`
# - action   — substrate `name args -> ret { \ }` typed lambda
# - grammar  — substrate `grammar @X(extensions) { ... }`
#
# The kind is the discriminator the shim's match cases over (cf.
# spec §10.1 dispatch table).
type declaration_kind = type_kind | prism_kind | action_kind | grammar_kind

# `declaration` — the input carrier the shim receives. A ref to the
# substrate declaration node (per `shards/mirror/store.mirror`'s
# `oid = ref` declaration), plus its discriminator. The shim is a
# function of the ref alone; the kind is materialized for the
# dispatch's convenience (the ref already encodes the kind, but
# carrying it explicitly makes the per-case typing legible).
type declaration = {
  oid: ref,
  kind: declaration_kind,
}

# === turn ===

# turn — a single move in the @code/metalogue conversation. Same
# shape as @metalogue.turn (shards/metalogue.mirror), lifted to the
# AST altitude: the speaker is a substrate ref; the body is a
# `declaration` (the AST utterance) rather than an `nl` term; the
# reply chain composes turns; the tick is the monotonic substrate
# clock per `boot/std/time.mirror`.
#
# The substrate utterance is the macro's input; the species'
# response is the emitted language AST; the conversation IS the
# closure of the four laws across the turn pair.
type turn = {
  speaker: ref,
  body: declaration,
  in_reply_to: option(turn),
  tick: tick,
}

# === metalogue_session ===

# metalogue_session — an ordered sequence of `@code/metalogue` turns
# plus the conversation's current opacity. Mirrors
# `@metalogue.metalogue_session` shape; the opacity carrier is the
# same @glass `transparency(turn)` parameterized on the lifted
# `turn` type. Every unresolved residual is sited on a turn — the
# residual opacity of the conversation IS the transparency of its
# turn-set.
type metalogue_session = {
  turns: [turn],
  opacity: transparency(turn),
}

# === The four shim signatures (universal contract) ===
#
# Per docs/specs/code-metalogue-surface.md §4.1, each
# `@code/X/macro` species realises four shims — one per declaration
# kind. The signatures are declared HERE at the ground because the
# contract is universal. Species bind the abstract return type to
# their language AST: `@code/rust/macro` binds `species_ast =
# code/rust.ast`, `@code/elixir/macro` would bind
# `species_ast = code/elixir.ast`, etc.
#
# `species_ast` is a type parameter at the ground — each species
# specializes it at its altitude. The substrate's grammar uses the
# parametric `(T)` shape to encode this (cf. `option(T)`, `result(T,
# E)` in boot/std/).
#
# The bodies are `\` at the ground per
# `[[feedback-craft-not-deliver]]`: the discipline is declared, the
# realisation per-species. Bodies discharge when a consumer pulls.

shim_type(d: declaration) -> species_ast { \ }
shim_prism(d: declaration) -> species_ast { \ }
shim_action(d: declaration) -> species_ast { \ }
shim_grammar(d: declaration) -> species_ast { \ }

# === project_hole — the universal hole-projection contract ===
#
# The 35th-instance recognition (2026-06-09, Alex → Mara via Reed):
# during T25 GREEN the substrate's `\` (the typed hole / obligation
# / unbound morphism per [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]])
# could not survive Rust source code at the proc-macro call site —
# Rust's lexer does not realise the bare `\` token. The interim
# workaround used `{ }` at the call site and emitted `todo!()` in
# the macro body. Mara surfaced the candidate naming "the lexical
# glass-wall" and flagged for review.
#
# Alex's read (verbatim): *"Yes, it needs an explicit mapping. No
# language has the exact semantics of the hole. It will always be
# a `project`ion."*
#
# The substrate-pull-correct recognition:
#
#   1. `\` IS the substrate primitive — the typed hole. The substrate
#      vocabulary is `hole`, not "lexical glass-wall" (per
#      shards/nl.mirror line 29: *"Same shape as `\` producing
#      fracture (an obligation hole)"*; per boot/std/mirror/interpreter.mirror's
#      `resolve_hole(hole, context) -> ast`; per boot/std/fate/tournament.mirror's
#      `candidates(hole)`; per boot/std/scheduler.mirror's "Fate-resolved
#      hole"). The substrate already had the word.
#
#   2. `project` IS the operation (one of the five-op operations on
#      `@code/metalogue` above, line 102). The hole-projection isn't
#      a new substrate operation; it's `project`'s contract at the
#      @code/metalogue altitude made explicit. `project` is what
#      carries the substrate AST (including its holes) across the
#      species boundary; each species realisation provides its
#      hole-projection as part of `project`'s body.
#
#   3. No species has exact-match semantics — Rust's `todo!()` is
#      close but isn't a typed-gap; `_` is a wildcard; `{ }` is an
#      empty block. Elixir, Lisp, Julia each project differently.
#      Every species lossy-projects. The mapping MUST be explicit
#      per species, declared at the species' `@code/X/macro`.
#
# `project_hole` is the universal contract at the ground:
#
#   - Input: the substrate `hole` carrier (the `\` body's typed
#     surface; the obligation-block carrier the dispatch reads when
#     `shim_action` encounters an action with `\` body).
#   - Output: the species AST fragment that realises the hole at
#     the species altitude. Parametric on `species_ast` per the
#     same mechanism as the four shims above.
#
# Each `shim_action` invocation MUST route through `project_hole`
# when its input declaration carries a `\` body. The four laws
# (round-trip, OID-functionality, type-soundness, substrate-pull
# preservation) apply identically — see the `requires` clauses
# added below.
#
# The substrate-vocabulary `hole` type is the @glass-altitude
# obligation surface; declared as an abstract type here because the
# floor (shards/glass.mirror) names `splinter` / `shard` / opacity
# but does not yet declare `hole` as a typed carrier. T26 may lift
# `type hole = { body: ref, signature: ref, location: location }`
# to @glass when a consumer pulls; this tick the type stays abstract
# and the action signature carries `hole` as the input ref kind.

project_hole(h: hole) -> species_ast { \ }

# === The four laws (per docs/specs/code-metalogue-surface.md §5) ===
#
# Each law applies to every `shim_X` above. Declared here ONCE at
# the ground; each species inherits the obligation via
# `<= @code/metalogue`. This is the load-bearing lift from
# per-species duplication: the laws are universal across every
# language altitude that admits direct AST manipulation.
#
# 1. Type-soundness: for every declaration D, the emitted
#    `species_ast` signature matches D's typed contract under the
#    species' `type_mapping(D.args, D.ret)`. The substrate's typed
#    contract IS the language's typed contract — the gap is typed.
#
# 2. Round-trip identity: for every D,
#    `parse_species(render_species(shim_X(D))) = shim_X(D)`
#    structurally. The macro emission is a fixed point of the
#    language-altitude parse cycle — the gap closes
#    deterministically. Same shape as the `round_trip(render)` law
#    declared on `@code/mirror.render` at `shards/code/mirror.mirror`
#    (T17, sibling altitude).
#
# 3. OID functionality: for substrate declarations D1, D2 with
#    `oid(D1) = oid(D2)`,
#    `oid(render_species(shim_X(D1))) = oid(render_species(shim_X(D2)))`.
#    The shim is a function of the substrate OID; same substrate
#    OID, same emitted OID — the gap is content-addressed.
#
# 4. Substrate-pull preservation: if D is substrate-realisable per
#    `@mirror/realisation.classify(D) = substrate(target)`, then
#    `shim_X(D)` references only language constructs that themselves
#    appear in some `@code/species` substrate-realisable shard. The
#    emission cannot reach into `@io` to fill a substrate-realisable
#    gap. The discriminator's verdict IS the discriminator of the
#    shim — the gap respects the glass wall.

requires round_trip(shim_type)
requires oid_function(shim_type)
requires type_soundness(shim_type)
requires substrate_pull_preserving(shim_type)

requires round_trip(shim_prism)
requires oid_function(shim_prism)
requires type_soundness(shim_prism)
requires substrate_pull_preserving(shim_prism)

requires round_trip(shim_action)
requires oid_function(shim_action)
requires type_soundness(shim_action)
requires substrate_pull_preserving(shim_action)

requires round_trip(shim_grammar)
requires oid_function(shim_grammar)
requires type_soundness(shim_grammar)
requires substrate_pull_preserving(shim_grammar)

# The four laws apply to `project_hole` as well — same shape,
# because the hole-projection IS the contract `shim_action` reduces
# to when its input action carries `\`. Round-trip: parsing the
# species' projected form yields back the hole carrier (up to the
# species' own lossy semantics; the partial verdict is admitted
# per @glass transparency). OID-functionality: same hole OID, same
# projected OID. Type-soundness: the species AST fragment matches
# the hole's declared signature under the species' type_mapping.
# Substrate-pull preservation: the projection does not silently
# absorb the gap — the species' lossy projection IS surfaced as
# transparency at the metalogue turn.
requires round_trip(project_hole)
requires oid_function(project_hole)
requires type_soundness(project_hole)
requires substrate_pull_preserving(project_hole)

out @code/metalogue
out declaration_kind
out declaration
out turn
out metalogue_session
out shim_type
out shim_prism
out shim_action
out shim_grammar
out project_hole
